     1,
     "<div>Hi Smile,</div><div> </div><div>上述內容似乎與參考資料習題相同, 請先參考 &quot;The Guide Version 2.5 , W. J. Owen, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, University of Richmond&quot; 內容先行練習, 有問題 post R code 再來討論.</div><div> </div><div>LINK:</div><div><a href=\"http://cran.r-project.org/doc/contrib/Owen-TheRGuide.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://cran.r-project.org/doc/contrib/Owen-TheRGuide.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793238911230000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0G_deFPLABsxInOgRfgFQh\">http://cran.r-project.org/doc/contrib/Owen-TheRGuide.pdf</a></div><div># end</div><div><br>Smile於 2013年1月7日星期一UTC+8下午5時10分23秒寫道：</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\" class=\"gmail_quote\"><p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\">1.The Fibonacci numbers are calculated by the following relation: F</font><sub><font size=\"2\">n</font></sub><font size=\"3\">=F</font><sub><font size=\"2\">n-1</font></sub><font size=\"3\">+F</font><sub><font size=\"2\">n-2</font></sub><font size=\"3\">, With F</font><sub><font size=\"2\">1</font></sub><font size=\"3\">= F</font><sub><font size=\"2\">2</font></sub><font size=\"3\"> =1.</font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\">(1) Compute the first 50 Fibonacci numbers; store the results in a vector (use R-command “vector” to create it). <i>You have to use a loop her</i></span><font face=\"新細明體\"><i><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\">e.</span></i><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"></span></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\">(2) For large n, the ratio F</font><sub><font size=\"2\">n</font></sub><font size=\"3\">/F</font><sub><font size=\"2\">n-1</font></sub><font size=\"3\"> approaches the “golden mean”:<span> </span></font></span><font face=\"新細明體\"><font size=\"3\"><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\">.</span><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"></span></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\">(3) What is the value of F</font><sub><font size=\"2\">50</font></sub><font size=\"3\">/F</font><sub><font size=\"2\">49</font></sub><font size=\"3\">; is it equal to the golden mean? </font></span></p>\r\n<div><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;;font-size:12pt\" lang=\"EN-US\">(4) When is n large enough? (i.e. sufficiently close (&lt;</span><font face=\"新細明體\"><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;;font-size:12pt\" lang=\"EN-US\">1</span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;;font-size:12pt\">×</span></font><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;;font-size:12pt\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">e<sup>-6</sup>) to the golden mean)</font></span></div>\r\n<div><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;;font-size:12pt\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"></font></span> </div>\r\n<div><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;;font-size:12pt\" lang=\"EN-US\">\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><font size=\"3\">2. Create a script file which draws a curve of the function y=x</font><sup><font size=\"2\">3</font></sup><font size=\"3\"> sin</font><sup><font size=\"2\">2</font></sup><font size=\"3\">(3π x) in the interval [-2, 2]. </font></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><font size=\"3\">Make a curve of the function y=1/cos(1+x</font><sup><font size=\"2\">2</font></sup><font size=\"3\">) in the interval [-5, 5]. </font></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"Times New Roman\"> </font></span></p><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"Times New Roman\">\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"\">3. Load in the<b><span style=\"color:blue\"> <span>stackloss</span></span><span> </span></b>dataset from within R</font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"\">(1) Save the variables <b><span style=\"color:blue\">Water.Temp</span> </b>and <b><span style=\"color:blue\">Acid.Conc</span></b><span>.</span> in a data frame called <b><span style=\"color:blue\">tempacid</span></b>.</font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">(2). Compute the mean, variance, and 5 number summary of the variable </span><b><span style=\"color:blue\" lang=\"EN-US\">stack.loss</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\">. </span></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"\">(3) Create a histogram, boxplot, and normal probability plot for the variable <b><span style=\"color:blue\">stack.loss</span></b>. Does an assumption of normality seem appropriate for this sample?</font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"\"> </font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"\">4. (1) Simulate 20 observations from the binomial distribution with <i>n</i>=15 and <i>p</i>=0.2.</font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"\">(2) Plot the Poisson mass function with λ = 4 over the range <i>x </i>= 0, 1, … 15.</font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"\"><font size=\"3\">(3) Simulate 100 observations from the normal distribution with μ = 50 and σ = 4. Plot the empirical cdf F</font><i><sub><font size=\"2\">n</font></sub></i><font size=\"3\">(<i>x</i>) for this sample and overlay the true CDF F(<i>x</i>). </font></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;;font-size:12pt\" lang=\"EN-US\">(4). Simulate 25 flips of a fair coin where the results are “heads” and “tails” (hint: use the <b><span style=\"color:blue\">sample()</span> </b>funct</span></p></font></span>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"Times New Roman\"></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><font size=\"3\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">5. </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">Data </span><b><span style=\"color:blue\" lang=\"EN-US\">airquality</span></b><span lang=\"EN-US\"> contains measurements of ozone concentration in New York from May to September 1983, together with other relevant variables.</span></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><font size=\"3\">(1) Plot the ozone concentration over time. Look at how different plotting types (&quot;l&quot;, &quot;h&quot;) affect the appearance. The EPA standard for ozone until recently was 120 ppb, 140 ppb was moderate nonattainment and 160 ppb serious nonattainment. Indicate these with horizontal lines in appropriate colors. Use the <b><span style=\"color:blue\">text()</span></b> function to annotate the severe ozone days with their dates.</font></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><font size=\"3\">(2) Ozone is produced by chemical reactions in the air that require sunlight. Draw a scatterplot of ozone and solar radiation. Is the relationship monotone?</font></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><font size=\"3\">(3) Perhaps wind or temperature is responsible for the shape of the plot. Use the <b><span style=\"color:blue\">coplot</span></b> function to draw scatterplots of ozone and solar radiation for different levels of wind speed, of temperature, and of both at once.</font></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"Times New Roman\"> </font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">6. <span style=\"color:black\">A famous data set that is part of R is the “</span></font></span><b><span style=\"color:blue;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\">iris</span></b><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\">” data set (Fisher, 1936), which gives measurements, in centimeters for sepal length and width and petal length and width, respectively, for 50 flowers of the species </span><span lang=\"EN-US\">Iris <span style=\"color:black\">setosa, Iris versicolor and Iris virginica. </span>The flowers are typically different shades of blue or purple and the plants are different sizes, but as with many plants there is enough color and size variation that these are not sufficient to classify the species. <i>I. setosa</i> is notable in that the ’standards’, the vertical sepals in an iris flower, are almost non-existent, while <i>I.</i><i> virginica</i> has the classic iris form.</span></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\">(1) What is the class of the data set? why?</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\">(2) What are the dimensions of the data set? (number of rows, columns)</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\">(3) Produce a scatter plot of petal length against petal width; produce an informative title and labels of the two axes.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">(4) Draw a histogram of sepal length. Are there visible subgroups?</font></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">(5) Plot sepal length and petal length for the irises, with different point colors for each species. This helps distinguish small flowers from large flowers with small sepals</font></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">(6) Do the same for petal length and width, and for sepal length and width.</font></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\">(7) Now produce a similar box-and whisker plot for all four morphological measurements, arranged in two rows and two columns. First specify the graphical parameter that arranges the plots two by two.</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\" face=\"Times New Roman\"> </font></span></b></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><b><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\">7. </font></span></b><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\">The saturated oxygen concentration in water (μmol kg</font><sup><font size=\"2\">-1</font></sup><font size=\"3\">), as function of temperature (T), and salinity (S) can be calculated by: SatOx = exp(A) </font></span></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:6pt 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">where : A= -173.9894 + 25559.07/T + 146.481</font></span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\">3</span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">×</span></font><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"><font size=\"3\">log</font><sub><font size=\"2\">e</font></sub><font size=\"3\">(T/100) -22.20</font></font></span><font size=\"3\"><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\">4</span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">×</span><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">T/100 + </font></span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\">S</span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">×</span><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">(-0.037362+0.01650</font></span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\">4</span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">×</span><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">T/100-0.002056</font></span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\">4</span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">×</span><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">T/10</font></span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\" lang=\"EN-US\">0</span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;\\0065b0\\007d30\\00660e\\009ad4&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">×</span><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">T/100) and T is temperature in Kelvin (T kelvin = T celsius+273.15). </font></span></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">(1) Make a function that implements this formula; the default values for temperature and salinity are 20°C and 35 respectively. </font></font></span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">(2) What is the saturated oxygen concentration at the default conditions? <i>(A: 225.2346) </i></font></font></span></p><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;;font-size:12pt\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">(3) Estimate the saturated oxygen concentration for a range of temperatures from 0 to 30°C, and salinity 35. <i>(Tip: no need to use loops)</i>.</font></span></span></div>\r\n<div><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;;font-size:12pt\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\"></font></span> </div>\r\n<div><span style=\"color:black;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;;font-size:12pt\" lang=\"EN-US\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\">請問上述需使用何種指令？</font></span></div></blockquote>"
    ]
   ]
  },
